THE AMAZING ADVENTURES OF KAVALIER AND CLAY by Michael Chabon

2000. Fourth Estate. Paperback.

More than usual this was a book I read in spurts. The prose is
extremely rich; I often feel that I'd like to re-read each page of a
book as I'm going (I never do, who's got the time?) but if any book
deserves reading in this fashion it's this one.

The book is well split into six parts; after I read part 1 I was able
to go to a movie of the Opera treatment from the New York Metropolitan
Opera; a very fine staging indeed and the music was nice enough.

The book is full of wonder and magic and is escapism of every kind
(the avenging Nazi-fighting comic-book hero Kavalier and Clay develop
is The Escapist).

The book ends with the Comic-Books-on-Trial hearings of 1954; which is
also well covered in the only full-length comic book I've read: Fanboy
by Sergio Aragone the margin cartoonist of Mad Magazine. In Kavalier
and Clay, Clay is made to testify at the comic books inquest and his
sexuality is publicly revealed which helps him live up to who he truly
is and leave his lavendar marriage to Joe Kavalier's girlfriend and
mother of his son.

The only other piece of literature I have on comics is V for Vendetta
as a collected  single volume; it's in the house somehwere and I keep
moving it around and misplacing it and then rediscovering it. I'll
read it next time I come across it and that will just about do it for
me and comics.

